```java
abstract class Report {
    private String title;         // fields allowed

    Report(String title) {        // constructors allowed
        this.title = title;
    }

    abstract void generateContent(); // abstract — subclass MUST implement
    abstract void formatOutput();    // abstract — subclass MUST implement

    final void generate() {          // concrete + final — template method
        generateContent();
        formatOutput();
        System.out.println("Report: " + title + " generated");
    }
}

class PDFReport extends Report {
    PDFReport() { super("PDF Report"); }
    void generateContent() { System.out.println("Generating PDF content"); }
    void formatOutput() { System.out.println("Formatting as PDF"); }
}

// new Report(); // COMPILE ERROR — cannot instantiate abstract class
Report r = new PDFReport(); // reference can be abstract type
r.generate();
```

This is the **Template Method pattern** — the abstract class defines the skeleton of an algorithm and lets subclasses fill in the specific steps.

```java
abstract class Shape {
    // Valid abstract method declarations:
    abstract double area();                    // package-private access
    public abstract String describe();         // public access
    protected abstract void draw(int x, int y); // protected access

    // INVALID — compile errors:
    // private abstract void hidden();  // private: can't be overridden
    // static abstract void util();     // static: belongs to class, not overridable
    // final abstract void fixed();     // final: can't be overridden
}

class Circle extends Shape {
    double radius;
    @Override public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }
    @Override public String describe() { return "Circle with r=" + radius; }
    @Override protected void draw(int x, int y) { /* draw at x,y */ }
    // Must implement ALL abstract methods or be abstract itself
}
```

The reasoning is logical: abstract methods exist to be **overridden**. `private` prevents overriding. `static` methods aren't polymorphic. `final` prevents overriding. All three are therefore incompatible with `abstract`.

Before Java 8, adding a method to an interface would break every class that implemented it. Java 8 solved this with default methods:

```java
interface Collection<E> {
    void add(E element);
    // Java 8: added without breaking existing implementations
    default Stream<E> stream() {
        return StreamSupport.stream(spliterator(), false);
    }
    static <T> void swap(List<T> list, int i, int j) { /* utility */ }
}
```

Implementing classes automatically inherit the default implementation. They can override it if needed.

**Diamond problem with defaults:**
```java
interface A { default void greet() { System.out.println("A"); } }
interface B { default void greet() { System.out.println("B"); } }

class C implements A, B {
    public void greet() { A.super.greet(); } // MUST override — resolves ambiguity
}
```

Java forces class `C` to resolve the conflict explicitly.

```java
abstract class Greeter {
    abstract String getMessage();
    void greet() { System.out.println(getMessage()); }
}

// Direct instantiation: COMPILE ERROR
// Greeter g = new Greeter();

// Normal usage: concrete subclass
class FormalGreeter extends Greeter {
    String getMessage() { return "Good day, sir."; }
}
Greeter g1 = new FormalGreeter(); // OK

// Anonymous class: instantiates in-place, provides abstract method impl
Greeter g2 = new Greeter() {  // technically creating an unnamed subclass
    String getMessage() { return "Hey!"; }
};
g2.greet(); // Hey!
```

The anonymous class syntax `new AbstractClass() { ... }` actually creates an unnamed concrete subclass on the fly and instantiates it. It's not the abstract class being instantiated — it's a new anonymous subclass. This pattern is common with `Runnable`, `Comparator`, and event listeners (though lambda expressions have largely replaced it since Java 8).

A functional interface has exactly one abstract method — making it possible to implement with a lambda expression.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface Validator<T> {
    boolean validate(T value); // the ONE abstract method
    // Can still have default methods:
    default Validator<T> and(Validator<T> other) {
        return value -> this.validate(value) && other.validate(value);
    }
}

// Lambda implements the single abstract method:
Validator<String> notBlank = s -> !s.isBlank();
Validator<String> maxLen = s -> s.length() <= 100;
Validator<String> combined = notBlank.and(maxLen);

System.out.println(combined.validate("Hello")); // true
```

**Built-in functional interfaces** in `java.util.function`:
- `Predicate<T>`: `boolean test(T t)` — used in `stream.filter()`
- `Function<T,R>`: `R apply(T t)` — used in `stream.map()`
- `Consumer<T>`: `void accept(T t)` — used in `stream.forEach()`
- `Supplier<T>`: `T get()` — provides a value lazily
